/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  postDict -- STAGE D.  THE BED THE ENGINE SIZED IS COSTED FROM ITS OWN VOLUME.

  The sequence's invariant -- A STAGE MAY NOT COST WHAT IT DID NOT SIZE --
  is what kept this file out of stage D until 2026-09-26, and it is what
  lets it in now: the converter's volume is `V_R`, the PFR's own KPI, SOLVED
  by system/outerDict's DesignSpec against the effluent's 5 K approach.  No
  volume is typed here.  The `pfr` sizer READS that KPI from the
  representative pass (the replay at the design point -- the pass the
  post-processing chain runs on under a `designSpec`, since 2026-09-26) and
  passes it through; its basis on the design sheet says "read from the unit"
  and nothing more, because the sizing pass has no view of the outer driver
  and does not pretend to know whether the number was solved or typed.  Here
  it was solved, and this header is where that is said.

  =========================================================================
  WHAT IS SIZED, WHAT IS COSTED, AND WHAT IS NOT
  =========================================================================
  The CONVERTER is TWO physical items under one unit (design/converter/):

    shell           the pressure vessel around the bed: a straight cylinder
                    of the bed's own volume at the declared L/D, ASME
                    thin-wall at pressureDesign.  COSTED as a `vessel` on the
                    Guthrie vessel set already in Choupo (Turton App. A), the
                    same way stage C, ammonia02 and the flagship cost theirs.
                    NO head space, distributor, support grid or internals: the
                    shell is the bed's envelope, not a vendor drawing.

    catalystCharge  the catalyst: m = V_R x catalystBulkDensity, priced at
                    catalystPrice x m in the run's own currency (EUR), today's
                    money.  NO index, NO material factor, NO module factor,
                    NO 1.18 -- the costing line prints the shape word
                    `declared-unit-price` and the price beside it so it cannot
                    be read as a Turton correlation.  THERE IS NO GUTHRIE SET
                    FOR A CATALYST BED and none was invented; remove
                    `catalystPrice` below and the run REFUSES the charge by
                    name and prints `TOTALS (EUR) -- INCOMPLETE`.

  Also sized and costed, exactly as stage C sizes them: the high-pressure
  SEPARATOR and the LET-DOWN drum (vapour residence time; vessel set).

  NOT sized, NOT costed: the preheater (`feedPreheat` is a `phaseChanger`, a
  duty with no exchanger geometry declared -- an area would need U and an
  approach nobody has stated for it), the mixer and the splitter (no
  equipment), and everything stage C's postDict lists as absent from this
  topology (no compressors, no feed/effluent interchanger).  This CAPEX is
  therefore PARTIAL IN UNITS as C's is, and the pellet effectiveness factor
  the README calls UNPRICED stays unpriced: the bed here is the fresh, clean,
  fully reduced bed of Eq 19, and a real charge is larger.

  =========================================================================
  THE TWO CATALYST NUMBERS ARE AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTIONS.  THOSE ARE THE WORDS.
  =========================================================================
  `catalystBulkDensity` and `catalystPrice` below were NOT read from a
  primary source that this file can cite: no vendor data sheet, paper or
  price list was retrieved for them, and a citation invented to dress a
  round number would convert `unsourced` into `falsely sourced`, which no
  reader and no gate can detect.  They are round, they are declared as
  assumptions in this comment, and they are the two numbers a reader
  replaces first -- with a catalyst vendor's bulk density (a dense
  magnetite-based charge is heavier than a light supported one; the number
  moves the charge mass one-for-one) and a quotation in the currency and
  year of the report.  Every other number in this file follows the same
  rule as stage C's: pressureDesign is the 200 bar loop plus a 10 % margin
  (220 bar, as C, ammonia02 and ammonia03 declare), and `L_over_D 6.0` is
  the tall-vessel ratio the same three cases use for their converters.

  `corrosionAllow` and `jointEfficiency` are DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED, as in
  stage C: the engine supplies both from `sizing/DesignDefaults`, announces
  each at its site and in the end-of-run caveat block, and lists them on the
  shell's sheet under `assumed ( ... )` -- the difference between a number
  this case chose and a number the engine chose, made visible.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
sizing
{
    units
    (
        //  THE KINETIC BED.  The volume is NOT here and no rule for it is:
        //  it is the unit's own solved V_R.  `type pfr` selects the
        //  catalyst-bed sizer (shell + catalystCharge).
        { unitName converter;  type pfr;  material SS316;
          designRules { pressureDesign       220.0;    // bar: 200 bar loop + 10 % margin
                        L_over_D             6.0;      // tall vessel, as stage C's converter
                        catalystBulkDensity  2500.0;   // kg/m3 -- AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION, see header
                        catalystPrice        15.0; } } // EUR/kg, today's money -- AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION, see header

        //  The high-pressure SEPARATOR: a knock-out drum, 45 s of vapour holdup.
        { unitName separator;  type vessel;  material SS316;
          designRules { residenceTime 45.0;  flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3.0;
                        pressureDesign 220.0; } }

        //  The product LET-DOWN drum at 20 bar: smaller, and carbon steel.
        { unitName letdown;    type vessel;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { residenceTime 60.0;  flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3.0;
                        pressureDesign 25.0; } }
    );
}
costing
{
    method    Turton;
    year      2026;
    cepci     820;
    cepci2001 397;
    usdToEur  0.92;
}
